@* The band across the top of a reference sheet: a tint the subject chooses, its name, a row of
   badges saying what it is, and the close button in the corner.

   The tint is either a class (TintClass — a flat wash from the palette) or a style
   (TintStyle — the gradient a spirit or board builds from its own colour, see
   GameLookups.TintBandStyle). Both spell the bottom border too, so the band's edge belongs to
   the tint rather than being a rule laid over it.

   Glyph is what identifies the subject beside its name — a colour bar, a lettered tile, an
   icon — and Badges the row under it. A sheet with neither still gets the same band. *@

@* The tint is one or the other, never both: a palette wash would show through the gradient's
   own transparency and muddy it. *@
<div class="sheet-head @(TintStyle is null ? TintClass ?? NeutralTint : null)" style="@TintStyle">
    <ModalCloseButton OnClick="OnClose" Floating="true"/>

    <div class="flex items-start gap-4 pr-10">
        @Glyph
        <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
            <h2 class="dialog-title-xl">@Title</h2>
            @if (Badges is not null)
            {
                <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-xs text-ink-600">@Badges</div>
            }
            @Extra
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A flat wash and its border, from the palette — a kind's tint (<c>.tint-saved</c>,
    /// <c>.tint-thematic</c>) or null for the neutral band.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TintClass { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The band a subject with no colour of its own wears.</summary>
    private const string NeutralTint = "border-ink-200 bg-ink-50/60";

    /// <summary>A tint the subject builds from its own colour — set instead of
    /// <see cref="TintClass"/>, and spell the border in it too.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TintStyle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What identifies the subject beside its name.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Glyph { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The badge row under the name — expansion, difficulty, whatever the sheet is about.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Badges { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Anything the band carries below the badges — the spirit sheet's elements and
    /// tokens, which are a legend rather than another badge.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Extra { get; set; }
}
